{% sec 1, "The Tape Is a Diary", "What can you learn from a list of trades?", "The tape is a diary" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Picture two sales at a fruit stall, both at £2 a box. In the first, a customer walks up and pays the price on the sign straight away. In the second, the stallholder, wanting to pack up, walks over to a shopper who'd said "I'd pay £2" and accepts. The till receipts look identical. But the first sale shows a buyer in a hurry and the second shows a seller in a hurry, and if you saw ten sales like the first in a row, you'd guess the stall was about to put its prices up.</p>
    <p>Markets keep a till roll too. The {% gl "tape" %}tape{% endgl %} is the sequence of every trade and every quote update. Deltashark calls it a record of everyone's actions, and actions leak intent. <strong>Order flow analysis</strong> uses simple counts and sums on the tape to estimate three things: which side is starting trades, whether pressure is building, and whether the flow looks informed. This note covers the first two. The third, telling informed flow from merely big flow, is the next note, Footprints of Size and Information.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 2, "Who Started It?", "How do you tell whether a trade was a buy or a sell?", "Who started it?" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Every trade has a buyer and a seller, so in one sense every trade is both. What matters is who was the {% gl "aggressor" %}aggressor{% endgl %}: the one who crossed the spread to trade immediately against an order that was already resting. Labelling each trade by its aggressor is called {% gl "trade signing" %}trade signing{% endgl %}, and it's the raw material for everything else in this chapter. There are three classic rules:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="numbers">
      <thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>How it signs a trade</th><th>Weak spot</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Quote rule</strong></td><td>At or near the ask → buy. At or near the bid → sell.</td><td>Can't sign a trade exactly at the midpoint</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>{% gl "tick test" %}Tick test{% endgl %}</strong></td><td>Higher than the last different trade price (an uptick) → buy. Lower (a downtick) → sell.</td><td>Ignores the quotes entirely</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>{% gl "lee–ready algorithm" %}Lee–Ready{% endgl %} (1991)</strong></td><td>Use the quote rule; for trades at the midpoint, fall back on the tick test.</td><td>Trades inside the spread (dark pools, hidden orders, price improvement)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>How often do they get it right? A well-known test on Nasdaq data where the true direction was known (Ellis, Michaely and O'Hara, 2000) found the quote rule right 76% of the time, the tick test 78% and Lee–Ready 81%. All three struggled most with trades inside the quotes, and those are now far more common than in 1991, because of {% gl "midpoint execution" %}midpoint trades{% endgl %} and sub-penny price improvement.</p>
    <p>The good news: many modern feeds don't make you guess. CME's market data, for example, tags every trade with an <strong>aggressor side</strong> (buy, sell, or none), so futures traders get signed trades for free. You still need the rules for US consolidated stock data, much over-the-counter data, and older historical datasets.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="lab">
    <div class="lab-head"><h3>Sign the tape</h3><p>Ten trades in one stock, each with the quote at the moment it printed. Decide whether a buyer or a seller started each one, then see what the rules say and what really happened. Your labels add up to an imbalance at the bottom.</p></div>
    <div class="tape" id="st-list"></div>
    <div class="readout" id="st-read"></div>
    <p class="look" id="st-story"></p>
    <div class="presets"><button type="button" id="st-reset">Start again</button></div>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 3, "Order Flow Imbalance", "How do you turn signed trades into one number for pressure?", "Order flow imbalance" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Once trades are signed, add them up. {% gl "signed volume" %}Signed volume{% endgl %} is buys minus sells. Divide by the total and you get Deltashark's {% gl "order flow imbalance" %}order flow imbalance{% endgl %} for an interval, a number between \(-1\) and \(+1\):</p>
  </div>
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">Order flow imbalance (Deltashark)</span>\[ \text{OFI} = \frac{V_{\text{buy}} - V_{\text{sell}}}{V_{\text{buy}} + V_{\text{sell}}} \]</div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>\(+1\) means every trade was buyer-initiated, \(-1\) every trade seller-initiated, and \(0\) balanced. Deltashark notes that short-horizon price changes line up strongly with imbalance in the same interval, across essentially every market.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="worked">
    <span class="lbl">Worked example (Deltashark): an interval on the tape</span>
    <h3>In five minutes, \(42{,}000\) shares trade at the ask and \(18{,}000\) at the bid. What is the imbalance, and how should you read it?</h3>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>\(\text{OFI} = (42 - 18)/(42 + 18) = 24/60 = 0.4\).</li>
      <li>Signed volume is \(+24{,}000\) shares: a strongly buy-imbalanced interval.</li>
      <li>Then check the book and the price: was depth at the offer used up faster than it was replaced? Did the mid move up with each burst of buying?</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="answer">If both are yes, you're watching demand meet limited supply: price impact happening in real time.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Traders also watch <strong>cumulative signed volume</strong>, the running total across intervals. Deltashark's second quick check is an example: \(+12{,}000\), \(+9{,}000\), \(+15{,}000\) and \(+11{,}000\) add up to \(+47{,}000\) shares of net buying. Four positive intervals in a row is <em>persistent</em> pressure, which matters for the next note: a single big order sliced into pieces tends to leave exactly this kind of run.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 4, "Imbalance and Price", "How much does the price move for a given imbalance?", "Imbalance and price" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Trades are only half the story. The book changes all the time without any trades: new limit orders join the best bid, others are cancelled from the best offer. Rama Cont, Arseniy Kukanov and Sasha Stoikov (published 2014) built an order flow imbalance that counts <em>all</em> of these events at the best prices, using quote and trade data for 50 US stocks:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Buying pressure: market buys, new bids at the best bid, and cancelled offers at the best ask.</li>
      <li>Selling pressure: market sells, new offers at the best ask, and cancelled bids at the best bid.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>They found that over short intervals, price changes are mainly driven by this imbalance, in a straight-line relationship whose slope is <strong>inversely proportional to depth</strong>. Thin books move a lot per share of imbalance; deep books barely move. And the relationship using trades alone was noisier and less reliable, because it misses all the quote activity.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">Price change against book imbalance (Cont, Kukanov and Stoikov)</span>\[ \Delta P \approx \beta \times \text{OFI}_{\text{book}} \qquad \beta \approx \frac{\delta}{2D} \]</div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Here \(\text{OFI}_{\text{book}}\) is in shares, not a ratio, \(\delta\) is the tick and \(D\) is the typical number of shares at the best prices. The \(\tfrac{\delta}{2D}\) comes from their simple model of the book; real slopes are estimated from data. The interactive shows both versions side by side.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="lab">
    <div class="lab-head"><h3>Imbalance and price</h3><p>Each dot is a 10-second interval: imbalance across, change in the mid price up. Switch between the full book imbalance and trades only, and change how deep the book is. The tone line is the best-fit slope; the dashed line is the simple model's \(\delta/2D\).</p></div>
    <div class="controls">
      <div class="presets rt-pick" id="ip-x" aria-label="Which imbalance"></div>
      <div class="ctrl"><label for="ip-d">Depth at best</label><input type="range" id="ip-d" min="500" max="8000" step="250" value="2000"><output id="ip-d-out" for="ip-d">2,000</output></div>
      <div class="ctrl"><label for="ip-q">Quote activity</label><input type="range" id="ip-q" min="0" max="2" step="0.1" value="1"><output id="ip-q-out" for="ip-q">×1.0</output></div>
      <div class="presets"><button type="button" id="ip-again">Another hour</button></div>
    </div>
    <svg class="chart" id="chart-ip" viewBox="0 0 640 300" role="img" aria-label="Scatter of order flow imbalance against change in mid price for 10-second intervals, with a fitted line"></svg>
