{% endsec %}

{% sec 5, "The Trap: Pressure Isn't Information", "If buying pressure moves prices, why not just follow it?", "Pressure isn't information" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Deltashark's warning: imbalance predicts <em>short-horizon</em> moves, but it doesn't say <em>why</em> the pressure is there. Treat pressure as information and you'll walk into every large uninformed rebalance ever executed. The month-end index flow is massively imbalanced and carries no view at all, and its price impact tends to reverse afterwards.</p>
    <p>Flow statistics classify pressure. Deciding whether that pressure carries information needs context the statistics don't have: the calendar (month-end, index changes, option expiry), the news, and whether related markets agree. That's the subject of the next note.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="big-idea">
    <span class="lbl">The big idea</span>
    <p>The tape tells you who's in a hurry and how hard they're pushing. It doesn't tell you whether they know something. Read the pressure, then go and find the reason.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 6, "And in FX, Gold and Bonds?", "Is there a tape to read in my three markets?", "FX, gold and bonds" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <h3>FX</h3>
    <p>Spot FX has no single public tape: most trading happens between banks, platforms and clients, and each sees only its own slice. Dealers read <em>their own</em> client flow, which is one reason big banks have an information edge in FX and why their "flow" commentary is valued. Outsiders can use currency futures, such as CME's euro contract, where every trade carries an aggressor flag, or buy aggregated volume and flow data from settlement providers such as CLS. Be careful with "volume" on retail FX platforms: it's usually a count of price changes at your broker, not real traded volume.</p>
    <h3>Gold</h3>
    <p>COMEX gold futures have a full signed tape. London's over-the-counter spot market doesn't publish individual trades, so the futures tape is where most gold order flow analysis happens.</p>
    <h3>Bonds</h3>
    <p>Treasury futures and the main interdealer platform, BrokerTec, give a real-time view of order flow. Cash Treasury trades have been reported to FINRA's TRACE system since July 2017, but only for regulators at first. Since 25 March 2024, FINRA has published individual trades in on-the-run notes and bonds at the end of each day. That's useful for research, but too late to trade on intraday, so the live tape for Treasuries is still the futures and BrokerTec.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 7, "Key Words", "What does the desk call these things?" %}
  <div class="terms">
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Lifted</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Your offer was taken by a buyer crossing the spread.</p><p class="eg">"Got lifted on 500 at 02."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Hit</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Your bid was taken by a seller crossing the spread.</p><p class="eg">"Someone hit the bid for 20 lots."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Paid</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>A buyer took the offer: the trade was buyer-initiated.</p><p class="eg">"Fifty paid, again and again."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Given</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>A seller took the bid: the trade was seller-initiated.</p><p class="eg">"Forty given, a thousand lots."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Print</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>A trade appearing on the tape.</p><p class="eg">"Big print at the bid just now."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Better buyer</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Buying pressure dominates the flow.</p><p class="eg">"Market's a better buyer since the open."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Absorbed</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Heavy aggressive flow that a big resting order soaks up without the price moving.</p><p class="eg">"They've absorbed every seller at 40."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Reload</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>A resting order that keeps reappearing at the same price after being filled.</p><p class="eg">"The offer keeps reloading, there's size behind it."</p></div>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 8, "How does this link to Trading, Real Estate and Fintech?", "Where reading order flow turns up in each part of the plan.", "How it links to the plan" %}
  <div class="link-block">
    <div class="link-block-head"><h3>Trading: what "volume delta" charts really show</h3><a class="theme-link tone-forest" href="/hub/trading/">Trading</a></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Many trading platforms sell "footprint" and "cumulative volume delta" charts. These are signed volume and cumulative signed volume, exactly this note's ideas. They're only as good as the data underneath: on gold or euro futures, the exchange's aggressor flags make them reliable; on spot FX at a retail broker, there's no real volume to sign at all. And remember the trap: a strong delta tells you who's pushing, not whether they're right. If you test a flow-based idea, check whether it still works on month-end days and index-rebalance days, when big uninformed flow dominates.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="link-block">
    <div class="link-block-head"><h3>Real estate: reading the property tape</h3><a class="theme-link tone-burgundy" href="/hub/real-estate/">Real Estate</a></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Housing has an order flow imbalance too. Surveys of estate agents, such as the RICS monthly survey in the UK, track new buyer enquiries against new properties coming to market. When enquiries rise and new listings fall, buyers outnumber sellers and prices tend to follow, just like a positive OFI. For a villa business, the equivalent is your own enquiry log: more serious enquiries than units available is a signal to firm up prices; the reverse is a signal to lean them down.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="link-block">
    <div class="link-block-head"><h3>Fintech and Nigeria: your app's own tape</h3><a class="theme-link tone-navy" href="/hub/fintech/">Fintech and Nigeria</a></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>A naira–dollar app sees every request to buy or sell dollars: that's a private, fully signed tape, which is exactly the advantage FX dealers have. Tracking net dollar demand each hour (an OFI) tells the app when to nudge its rate before its dollar inventory runs out, and comparing its own imbalance with moves in the wider market shows whether its users are early (informed) or just noisy. Keep the trap in mind: salary days, school-fee season and month-end import payments create huge one-sided flow that says nothing about where the naira is going.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 9, "The Maths Underneath", "How do you compute the book imbalance and its price impact?" %}
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">Signed volume and the volume imbalance over an interval</span>\[ \begin{gathered} S = \sum_{i} s_i\,v_i \qquad s_i = \begin{cases} +1 & \text{buyer-initiated} \\ -1 & \text{seller-initiated} \end{cases} \\[6pt] \text{OFI} = \frac{S}{\sum_i v_i} \end{gathered} \]</div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The book version adds up changes at the best bid and best ask between one snapshot and the next. In words: more size on the bid, or less on the ask, is buying pressure. If the bid price rises, the whole new bid counts; if it falls, the whole old bid counts against. The ask works the other way round.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">Book imbalance between two snapshots (Cont, Kukanov and Stoikov)</span>\[ \begin{gathered} e = \Delta(\text{bid side}) - \Delta(\text{ask side}) \\[6pt] \text{same prices: } e = (q^{b}_{\text{new}} - q^{b}_{\text{old}}) - (q^{a}_{\text{new}} - q^{a}_{\text{old}}) \end{gathered} \]</div>
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">Fitting the slope: least squares over many intervals</span>\[ \begin{gathered} \hat\beta = \frac{\sum_k x_k\,y_k}{\sum_k x_k^{2}} \\[6pt] R^{2} = 1 - \frac{\sum_k (y_k - \hat\beta x_k)^{2}}{\sum_k y_k^{2}} \end{gathered} \]</div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>\(x_k\) is the imbalance in interval \(k\) and \(y_k\) the change in the mid. \(R^{2}\) is the share of price moves the imbalance explains: close to \(1\) means imbalance explains almost everything, close to \(0\) means almost nothing.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="worked">
    <span class="lbl">Worked example</span>
    <h3>The best bid stays at \(50.00\) and its size grows from \(500\) to \(800\). The best ask stays at \(50.01\) and its size falls from \(400\) to \(300\). Depth is typically \(2{,}000\) shares. What's the book imbalance, and the predicted move?</h3>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Bid side: \(800 - 500 = +300\). Ask side: \(300 - 400 = -100\).</li>
      <li>\(e = +300 - (-100) = +400\) shares of buying pressure.</li>
      <li>\(\beta \approx \tfrac{1}{2 \times 2{,}000} = 0.00025\) ticks per share, so \(\Delta P \approx 400 \times 0.00025 = 0.1\) ticks.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="answer">No trade happened, yet the book leaned towards buying. That's exactly the information a trades-only measure misses.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 10, "Practice", "Have a go, then open the answer. These double as interview questions." %}
  <div class="practice">
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">1</span><span>(Deltashark quick check) An interval prints \(30{,}000\) shares at the ask and \(50{,}000\) at the bid. What is the order flow imbalance?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>\((30{,}000 - 50{,}000)/80{,}000 = -0.25\).</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">2</span><span>(Deltashark quick check) Signed volume runs \(+12{,}000\), \(+9{,}000\), \(+15{,}000\), \(+11{,}000\) across four intervals. What is total net signed volume?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>\(+47{,}000\) shares.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">3</span><span>The quote is \(20.10\) / \(20.12\). A trade prints at \(20.11\), and the last different trade price was \(20.12\). How does Lee–Ready sign it?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>It's at the midpoint, so the quote rule can't decide. The tick test sees a downtick from \(20.12\), so it's signed as a sell.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">4</span><span>Best bid \(99.50\) for \(1{,}000\) → still \(99.50\) for \(600\). Best ask \(99.51\) for \(700\) → still \(99.51\) for \(900\). What's the book imbalance?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>Bid side \(-400\), ask side \(+200\): \(e = -400 - 200 = -600\) shares, selling pressure.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">5</span><span>A stock usually shows \(5{,}000\) shares at the best prices. Using \(\beta \approx \delta/2D\), how many ticks would \(+3{,}000\) shares of book imbalance move the mid?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>\(3{,}000 / (2 \times 5{,}000) = 0.3\) ticks.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">6</span><span>Interview question: you have trades and quotes for a stock but no aggressor flags. How would you work out signed volume, and what could go wrong?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>I'd use Lee–Ready: the quote rule (at or near the ask is a buy, at or near the bid a sell), with the tick test for trades at the midpoint. I'd need the quote in force just before each trade, so timestamps have to line up. It goes wrong mostly for trades inside the spread, such as midpoint dark trades and price-improved retail orders, which are now common; studies found the rules right only about 76–81% of the time even in older data. If I could, I'd use a venue feed with aggressor flags instead.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">7</span><span>Interview question: order flow imbalance has been strongly positive in a stock for twenty minutes. Do you buy?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>Not on that alone. Imbalance tells me there's buying pressure and predicts the next few seconds, but not why. I'd check the context: is it month-end or an index change, when big uninformed flows are one-sided? Is there news? Are related instruments (the sector, the index future) moving the same way? If the pressure is a rebalance, its impact usually fades and buying into it means paying up just before it reverses. If it's confirmed across markets near a news event, it's more likely to be informed.</span></div></details></div>
  </div>
